  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nEarly this morning, the stationary front that brought widespread\nlight rainfall yesterday has finally started to push eastward\nand resulted in all rainfall now well southeast of the Northland.\nSatellite imagery shows slowly expanding very low-based stratus\nsky cover that will linger in these overnight hours, creating \npockets of half-mile visibility in fog through the morning \ncommute hours. Dense fog is not currently forecasted in most \nareas, but we will monitor observation trends for any potential \nheadline needs towards the dawn hours. This stratus will slowly \nclear late this morning into mid-day today. Once the sun breaks \nout, expect dry weather today under seasonably very warm \ntemperatures. Highs will reach the 70s across the Northland, \nwith pockets approaching 80 degrees for northwest Wisconsin.\n\nA stout dry cold frontal passage is on track for late tonight into\nearly Thursday morning. This front creates a wind shift to the\nnorthwest and ushers in a notably cooler air mass for Thursday.\nHigh temperatures will drop 10 to 15 degrees Thursday compared to\ntoday, but it will actually feel fairly normal for very early \nOctober. Along with the cooler air, expect breezy northwest \nwinds Thursday gusting 20 to 30 mph mainly in far northern\nMinnesota, more likely only up to 20 mph along and south of US\nHwy 2 in the rest of the region.\n\nThe winds will relax as high pressure settles in, setting the stage\nfor a chilly end to the work week. There is a fairly widespread\nfrost likelihood Thursday night into Friday morning, and \nheadlines are expected to be needed in time. Pockets of freezing\ntemperatures are anticipated mainly for the Minnesota Borderlands\nto the Iron Range and inland northwest Wisconsin. Dry weather \ncontinues through Friday night as the region remains under the \ninfluence of the surface high pressure system.\n\nAn incoming shortwave trough will move first into north-central\nMinnesota Saturday morning, then spread across northeast \nMinnesota to northwest Wisconsin by Saturday afternoon and \nevening. Precipitation types remain as all rainfall and brings \nup to a quarter inch of new rain.\n\nBeyond the rainfall Saturday, the other main concern will be a\nfrost potential that lasts each night this weekend. However, \nthe threat becomes more widespread again with even better \nchances of sub-freezing temperatures for Sunday night into \nMonday morning. A return to warm air advection sets up Monday, \nallowing temperatures to warm again by Tuesday, and especially \nby mid-next week when highs climb back to well-above normal.",
